# Architecture — custom store, not Redux/Zustand

## Context

`app/` uses a small in-house store (`app/src/store.ts`), not Redux, Zustand,
MobX, or Jotai. `createStore()` returns `{ getState, dispatch, subscribe }` and
that is the **only** sanctioned way state changes. New behavior is added by
extending the `Action` discriminated union in `app/src/types.ts` and handling
the new variant in `app/src/reducer.ts` — this is the "golden path" described
in `materials/architecture-brief.md`.

## Rule

- All state changes go through `store.dispatch(action)`. Never mutate
  `state.tasks` or `state.filter` directly, and never call `reducer()` outside
  the store.
- To add new behavior: add an `Action` variant in `app/src/types.ts` → handle
  it in `app/src/reducer.ts` → add an action creator in `app/src/actions.ts` →
  add a colocated test.
- Do NOT introduce Redux, Zustand, MobX, Jotai, or any other state-management
  library. Do NOT "modernize" `createStore`/`dispatch`/`subscribe` by replacing
  them with a library-based implementation.
- Reads go through `app/src/selectors.ts` helpers, not raw `state.tasks`
  access, when adding new UI/consumer code.

## How to verify

1. `rg -n "redux|zustand|mobx|jotai" app/package.json app/src --glob '!*.test.ts'`
   — no matches.
2. `rg -n "state\.(tasks|filter)\s*=|state\.tasks\s*\[[^\]]+\]\s*=|state\.tasks\.(push|splice)\s*\(" app/src --glob '!*.test.ts'`
   — no matches in production code.
3. To catch mutations through local aliases in `reducer.ts` and related
   production code, run an AST check such as
   `npx eslint app/src --ext .ts --ignore-pattern '*.test.ts' --rule 'no-param-reassign: error'`
   or an equivalent TypeScript AST scan for assignments/update expressions and
   mutating calls (`push`, `splice`) whose target resolves back to `state`,
   `state.tasks`, or a local alias derived from them. Test fixtures are allowed
   to construct mutable-looking objects; production code is not.
4. Any new `Action` variant added to `types.ts` has a matching `case` in
   `reducer.ts` and a matching creator in `actions.ts`.
